==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|reportor}} {{qualifier|obsolete}}
* {{l|en|reportour}} {{qualifier|obsolete}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|reportour}}, from {{der|en|fro|reporteur}}; equivalent to {{affix|en|report|-er|id2=agent noun}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|GenAm}} {{IPA|en|/ɹɪˈpɔɹtɚ/}}
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ɹɪˈpɔːtə/}}
* {{a|rhotic|horse-hoarse}} {{IPA|en|/ɹɪˈpo(ː)ɹtɚ/}}
* {{a|nonrhotic|horse-hoarse}} {{IPA|en|/ɹɪˈpoətə/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-reporter.ogg|Audio (US)}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɔː(ɹ)tə(ɹ)|s=3}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# Someone or something that reports.
#: {{ux|en|The '''reporters''' of important security bugs may be paid a bounty by the software developer.}}
# A [[journalist]] who [[investigate]]s, [[edit]]s and [[report]]s [[news]] [[stories]] for [[newspaper]]s, [[radio]] and [[television]].
# A person who [[record]]s and [[issue]]s [[official]] reports of [[judicial]] or [[legislative]] [[proceedings]].
# {{lb|en|legal}} A [[case reporter]]; a bound volume of printed [[legal]] [[opinion]]s from a particular [[jurisdiction]].
# {{lb|en|biology}} A [[gene]] attached by a [[researcher]] to a [[regulatory]] [[sequence]] of another gene of interest, typically used as an indication of whether a certain gene has been taken up by or [[express]]ed in the cell or organism population.

====Derived terms====
{{col3|en|case reporter|court reporter|cub reporter|law reporter|police reporter}}

